objc-sys (the runtime bindings under objc2 0.5) on powerpc-apple-darwin
(Mac OS X 10.4 to 10.6, legacy ObjC runtime).

libobjc on 10.6 and earlier has none of the ARC entry points (objc_retain,
objc_release, objc_autorelease*, objc_autoreleasePoolPush/Pop,
objc_storeStrong, objc_retainBlock, the weak functions) and no
objc_copyClassList; they arrived in 10.7. On powerpc, src/legacy.rs replaces
src/rc.rs and implements them with -retain/-release/-autorelease,
NSAutoreleasePool, _Block_copy and objc_getClassList. There are no zeroing
weak references either, so weak references are strong ones on powerpc: no
dangling, but a cycle a weak reference was meant to break leaks.

The `unstable-exception` helper (extern/exception.m) retains the caught
exception with a -retain message on PowerPC for the same reason.

